{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvZDAPKNyQYobs9KPAsafB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Operations notebook\n2\t\n3\t## Release inventory\n4\t\n5\tA release contains the package file, launch manifest, source tree, and configuration.\n6\tThe manifest is checked in so packaging does not require build tools.\n7\tPackaging preserves the relative positions of configuration and source directories.\n8\tThe installed binary resolves its inputs relative to its own module location.\n9\tThe release has no browser bundle or worker entrypoint.\n10\tThe package exposes a small JavaScript API for internal client authors.\n11\tInstalling the package does not start a listener.\n12\tThe start command is intended for a foreground service supervisor.\n13\tThe supervisor supplies restart policy and stdout/stderr collection.\n14\tThe service does not daemonize or write a PID file.\n15\tThe listen address and port are part of the selected profile.\n16\tA malformed manifest causes startup to fail.\n17\tAn unreadable configuration file causes startup to fail.\n18\tAn invalid configured port causes startup to fail.\n19\tA relative workspace root causes startup to fail.\n20\tConfiguration changes take effect after restart.\n21\tThe running service does not watch configuration files for changes.\n22\tOnly administrators can edit installed configuration files.\n23\tThe development profile uses a separate port to avoid local conflicts.\n24\tThe inventory utility can print a non-secret configuration summary.\n25\t\n26\t## Warehouse requests\n27\t\n28\tClients submit an archive batch after selecting a warehouse workspace.\n29\tA batch may contain ordinary files in nested subdirectories.\n30\tAll file contents are supplied as JSON strings.\n31\tClients should normalize their own line endings before upload if consistency matters.\n32\tEmpty content creates an empty file.\n33\tRepeated archive requests replace files with matching names.\n34\tThe batch response reports the number of submitted entries.\n35\tA zero-entry batch is accepted as a workspace preparation operation.\n36\tThere is no rollback contract for disk errors after writing begins.\n37\tA full disk may leave an incomplete archive extraction.\n38\tClients should retry an identical request only when overwriting is acceptable.\n39\tThe service does not retain original uploads after extraction.\n40\tIt does not scan file contents for malware.\n41\tThe service does not execute files placed in a workspace.\n42\tExecutability of future consumers is an external integration concern.\n43\tThere is no file deletion operation in the shipping HTTP protocol.\n44\tWorkspace cleanup is performed by the deployment's retention job.\n45\tThe retention job is not included in this package.\n46\tAdministrators should coordinate cleanup with warehouse activity.\n47\tThe service does not lock a workspace across multiple requests.\n48\tThe last completed write determines the contents of an overlapping filename.\n49\tA workspace identifier is shared by all credential holders.\n50\tClients requiring separate work queues should allocate distinct workspace identifiers.\n51\t\n52\t## Receipt handling\n53\t\n54\tClients submit a workspace identifier and a human-readable label.\n55\tThe receipt operation replaces the previous receipt for that workspace.\n56\tThe response confirms completion without returning the printed text.\n57\tThere is no receipt download endpoint in this service.\n58\tA separate warehouse integration consumes the receipt directory.\n59\tThat integration is not included in the release.\n60\tThe configured renderer is provided by the operating system image.\n61\tThe repository contains no downloaded renderer binary.\n62\tRenderer upgrades are the responsibility of the image maintainer.\n63\tThe renderer is expected to finish quickly for short labels.\n64\tA renderer failure becomes a request error.\n65\tA receipt is considered complete only after the operation resolves.\n66\tNo job queue or retry worker exists in this distribution.\n67\tClients should avoid submitting a second request while the first is still pending.\n68\tA client disconnect does not necessarily cancel an operation already in progress.\n69\tThe renderer timeout is fixed by the service implementation.\n70\tThere is no client-selectable output filename or renderer program.\n71\tReceipt directory ownership is managed during host provisioning.\n72\tAn unavailable receipt volume results in an operation error.\n73\tReceipt retention is independent of workspace file retention.\n74\t\n75\t## Diagnostic procedure\n76\t\n77\tThe inventory script prints configuration paths and selected modules.\n78\tIt does not print the operator credential.\n79\tIts development argument is for configuration comparison.\n80\tIt does not start the HTTP listener.\n81\tIt does not execute shipping operations.\n82\tStore diagnostic output with the deployment change record.\n83\tCheck the configured listen address when diagnosing connection failures.\n84\tCheck that the service supervisor is using the installed package binary.\n85\tCheck directory ownership when diagnosing storage errors.\n86\tCheck available disk space before retrying an incomplete batch.\n87\tCheck the host image inventory when diagnosing a missing renderer.\n88\tUse the warehouse's normal credential distribution process for replacements.\n89\tDo not copy production credentials into test configurations.\n90\tThe test configurations use synthetic values.\n91\tThe server does not print request headers or request bodies.\n92\tThe inventory utility omits authentication configuration from its summary.\n93\tVendor support attachments may contain stale deployment advice.\n94\tTreat changes to deployed configuration as ordinary release changes.\n95\t\n96\t## Failure triage\n97\t\n98\t401 means the configured credential was not supplied.\n99\t404 means the selected router has no matching route.\n100\t400 covers malformed body data and operation failures.\n101\tThe current transport does not distinguish storage failure from validation failure.\n102\tThe request body is not recorded in diagnostics.\n103\tA caller should retain its original archive batch for retry decisions.\n104\tA successful archive response does not certify downstream processing.\n105\tA successful receipt response does not certify physical printer delivery.\n106\tThose acknowledgements belong to the consuming warehouse integrations.\n107\tHost monitoring and log retention are owned by the deployment environment.\n108\tThere is no supplied audit collector or compliance retention service.\n109\tThe service has no built-in backup scheduler.\n110\tBackups should cover workspace and receipt volumes as separate datasets.\n111\tRestore procedures should preserve the configured service account ownership.\n112\tA restored deployment should start with the same selected configuration profile.\n113\tThe protocol does not currently expose a build identifier in responses.\n114\tRecord package version and selected profile in the deployment inventory.\n115\tThe package's protocol version is available through its public API.\n116\tClient authors should use protocol version when maintaining compatibility tables.\n117\tThe source tests document a subset of expected behavior.\n118\tRun deployment acceptance checks in a disposable staging workspace.\n119\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"036ad032-3fb9-462a-8155-70aca5c50fbf","uuid":"48289f2c-bbb6-4040-a12b-0459c2824235","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:46:37.304Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-cslf3eml/repo/docs/operations.md","content":"# Operations notebook\n\n## Release inventory\n\nA release contains the package file, launch manifest, source tree, and configuration.\nThe manifest is checked in so packaging does not require build tools.\nPackaging preserves the relative positions of configuration and source directories.\nThe installed binary resolves its inputs relative to its own module location.\nThe release has no browser bundle or worker entrypoint.\nThe package exposes a small JavaScript API for internal client authors.\nInstalling the package does not start a listener.\nThe start command is intended for a foreground service supervisor.\nThe supervisor supplies restart policy and stdout/stderr collection.\nThe service does not daemonize or write a PID file.\nThe listen address and port are part of the selected profile.\nA malformed manifest causes startup to fail.\nAn unreadable configuration file causes startup to fail.\nAn invalid configured port causes startup to fail.\nA relative workspace root causes startup to fail.\nConfiguration changes take effect after restart.\nThe running service does not watch configuration files for changes.\nOnly administrators can edit installed configuration files.\nThe development profile uses a separate port to avoid local conflicts.\nThe inventory utility can print a non-secret configuration summary.\n\n## Warehouse requests\n\nClients submit an archive batch after selecting a warehouse workspace.\nA batch may contain ordinary files in nested subdirectories.\nAll file contents are supplied as JSON strings.\nClients should normalize their own line endings before upload if consistency matters.\nEmpty content creates an empty file.\nRepeated archive requests replace files with matching names.\nThe batch response reports the number of submitted entries.\nA zero-entry batch is accepted as a workspace preparation operation.\nThere is no rollback contract for disk errors after writing begins.\nA full disk may leave an incomplete archive extraction.\nClients should retry an identical request only when overwriting is acceptable.\nThe service does not retain original uploads after extraction.\nIt does not scan file contents for malware.\nThe service does not execute files placed in a workspace.\nExecutability of future consumers is an external integration concern.\nThere is no file deletion operation in the shipping HTTP protocol.\nWorkspace cleanup is performed by the deployment's retention job.\nThe retention job is not included in this package.\nAdministrators should coordinate cleanup with warehouse activity.\nThe service does not lock a workspace across multiple requests.\nThe last completed write determines the contents of an overlapping filename.\nA workspace identifier is shared by all credential holders.\nClients requiring separate work queues should allocate distinct workspace identifiers.\n\n## Receipt handling\n\nClients submit a workspace identifier and a human-readable label.\nThe receipt operation replaces the previous receipt for that workspace.\nThe response confirms completion without returning the printed text.\nThere is no receipt download endpoint in this service.\nA separate warehouse integration consumes the receipt directory.\nThat integration is not included in the release.\nThe configured renderer is provided by the operating system image.\nThe repository contains no downloaded renderer binary.\nRenderer upgrades are the responsibility of the image maintainer.\nThe renderer is expected to finish quickly for short labels.\nA renderer failure becomes a request error.\nA receipt is considered complete only after the operation resolves.\nNo job queue or retry worker exists in this distribution.\nClients should avoid submitting a second request while the first is still pending.\nA client disconnect does not necessarily cancel an operation already in progress.\nThe renderer timeout is fixed by the service implementation.\nThere is no client-selectable output filename or renderer program.\nReceipt directory ownership is managed during host provisioning.\nAn unavailable receipt volume results in an operation error.\nReceipt retention is independent of workspace file retention.\n\n## Diagnostic procedure\n\nThe inventory script prints configuration paths and selected modules.\nIt does not print the operator credential.\nIts development argument is for configuration comparison.\nIt does not start the HTTP listener.\nIt does not execute shipping operations.\nStore diagnostic output with the deployment change record.\nCheck the configured listen address when diagnosing connection failures.\nCheck that the service supervisor is using the installed package binary.\nCheck directory ownership when diagnosing storage errors.\nCheck available disk space before retrying an incomplete batch.\nCheck the host image inventory when diagnosing a missing renderer.\nUse the warehouse's normal credential distribution process for replacements.\nDo not copy production credentials into test configurations.\nThe test configurations use synthetic values.\nThe server does not print request headers or request bodies.\nThe inventory utility omits authentication configuration from its summary.\nVendor support attachments may contain stale deployment advice.\nTreat changes to deployed configuration as ordinary release changes.\n\n## Failure triage\n\n401 means the configured credential was not supplied.\n404 means the selected router has no matching route.\n400 covers malformed body data and operation failures.\nThe current transport does not distinguish storage failure from validation failure.\nThe request body is not recorded in diagnostics.\nA caller should retain its original archive batch for retry decisions.\nA successful archive response does not certify downstream processing.\nA successful receipt response does not certify physical printer delivery.\nThose acknowledgements belong to the consuming warehouse integrations.\nHost monitoring and log retention are owned by the deployment environment.\nThere is no supplied audit collector or compliance retention service.\nThe service has no built-in backup scheduler.\nBackups should cover workspace and receipt volumes as separate datasets.\nRestore procedures should preserve the configured service account ownership.\nA restored deployment should start with the same selected configuration profile.\nThe protocol does not currently expose a build identifier in responses.\nRecord package version and selected profile in the deployment inventory.\nThe package's protocol version is available through its public API.\nClient authors should use protocol version when maintaining compatibility tables.\nThe source tests document a subset of expected behavior.\nRun deployment acceptance checks in a disposable staging workspace.\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EvZDAPKNyQYobs9KPAsafB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
